  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(54pt, 389.4pt, "Existing digital manufacturing methods can be broadly divided into subtractive approaches, where ")
  #line-at(54pt, 403.2pt, "material is removed from a bulk to reveal the desired form, and additive methods, in which material is ")
  #line-at(54pt, 417pt, "introduced voxel-by-voxel to create an object. ")
  #line-at(54pt, 444.6pt, "We here show a fundamentally different method for the fabrication of three-dimensional objects that is ")
  #line-at(54pt, 458.4pt, "neither subtractive nor additive. Instead of removal or layer-by-layer material deposition, in LiquiFab we ")
  #line-at(54pt, 472.2pt, "shape an entire volume of liquid polymer by subjecting it to a set of geometrical constraints under ")
  #line-at(54pt, 486pt, "conditions of weightlessness. The physics of liquid interfaces then drives the polymer to naturally adopt ")
  #line-at(54pt, 499.8pt, "a configuration that minimizes its surface energy. On Earth, we achieve weightlessness through neutral ")
  #line-at(54pt, 513.6pt, "buoyancy, and show that a small, well-defined set of boundary surfaces can be used to drive the liquid ")
  #line-at(54pt, 527.4pt, "into a desired form that is then solidified. By sequentially applying this process, complex architectures ")
  #line-at(54pt, 541.2pt, "can be assembled from successive liquid-formed elements. ")
  #line-at(54pt, 568.8pt, "Unlike additive manufacturing, where every point within the object must be individually visited by a print ")
  #line-at(54pt, 582.6pt, "head or light field, LiquiFab forms the entire structure simultaneously. This makes the process highly ")
  #line-at(54pt, 596.4pt, "scalable and opens the door to rapid manufacturing of large objects both on Earth and in space. ")
]

