  #line-at(54pt, 83.16pt, "Introduction ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(54pt, 110.76pt, "Form-finding is an approach in which the shape of a surface or structure is defined by a natural ")
  #line-at(54pt, 124.56pt, "phenomenon that manifests itself in three dimensions (e.g., soap bubbles) or has a three-dimensional ")
  #line-at(54pt, 138.36pt, "representation (e.g., energy level around an atom). The designer cannot directly alter the underlying ")
  #line-at(54pt, 152.16pt, "physics, but can nonetheless affect the form by controlling a set of parameters or boundary conditions.")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(545.52pt, 147.6pt, "1")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(54pt, 179.76pt, "One of the prominent examples of form finding dates back to the art nouveau architect Antoni Gaudí in ")
  #line-at(54pt, 193.56pt, "the late 19th century, who suspended interconnected chains from fixed points at different distances and ")
  #line-at(54pt, 207.36pt, "hung weights (sandbags) at various locations along the chains. Under gravity, the chains take a ")
  #line-at(54pt, 221.16pt, "discontinuous catenary shape, which Gaudí replicated in his structures using standard construction ")
  #line-at(54pt, 234.96pt, "techniques.")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(108.24pt, 230.4pt, "2")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(112.32pt, 234.96pt, " Starting in the early 1960s, architect Frei Otto explored the creation of surfaces by subjecting ")
  #line-at(54pt, 248.76pt, "soap bubbles to various spatial boundary conditions.")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(307.44pt, 244.2pt, "3")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(311.52pt, 248.76pt, " Similar to Gaudi, his exploration was used only in ")
  #line-at(54pt, 262.56pt, "the design process, and the actual structures were implemented using existing construction techniques of ")
  #line-at(54pt, 276.36pt, "the time. Form-Finding can also occur digitally, as first demonstrated in the 1990s by architect Greg ")
  #line-at(54pt, 290.16pt, "Lynn")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(79.32pt, 285.6pt, "4")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(83.4pt, 290.16pt, ". He predicted new forms using a 3D computational ‘Blobiness’ model originally developed by ")
  #line-at(54pt, 303.96pt, "James Blinn")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(113.04pt, 299.4pt, "5")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(117.12pt, 303.96pt, " for graphically describing electron clouds in an atom.  ")
  #line-at(54pt, 331.56pt, "Recently, Frumkin and Bercovici")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(217.92pt, 327pt, "6")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(222pt, 331.56pt, " and Elgarisi et al")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(311.28pt, 327pt, "7")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(315.36pt, 331.56pt, ", developed a new fabrication method for optical ")
  #line-at(54pt, 345.36pt, "components that is based on a volume of liquid polymer naturally finding its form when coming in contact ")
  #line-at(54pt, 359.16pt, "with a bounding frame under weightlessness conditions. The designer cannot change the laws of physics ")
  #line-at(54pt, 372.96pt, "governing the shape, but can modify the bounding frame and liquid volume to obtain a desired optical ")
  #line-at(54pt, 386.76pt, "function. The authors showed the implementation of this method both in space")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(424.44pt, 382.2pt, "8")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(428.52pt, 386.76pt, " and on Earth, using neutral ")
  #line-at(54pt, 400.56pt, "buoyancy conditions to counter gravity.  ")
  #line-at(54pt, 428.16pt, "We here expand this approach into a more general form-finding fabrication method, which we term ")
  #line-at(54pt, 441.96pt, "LiquiFab. LiquiFab creates three-dimensional objects by bringing a liquid volume into contact with a ")
  #line-at(54pt, 455.76pt, "small set of surfaces purposely positioned in space. In contrast to 3D printing, where objects are built by ")
  #line-at(54pt, 469.56pt, "following a toolpath (FDM)")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(192.12pt, 465pt, "9")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(196.2pt, 469.56pt, " or by sequentially solidifying layers (SLA or DLP)")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(454.32pt, 465pt, "10")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(462.36pt, 469.56pt, ", LiquiFab is scale-")
  #line-at(54pt, 483.36pt, "invariant and produces the final object instantaneously once the injected liquid comes into contact with its ")
  #line-at(54pt, 497.16pt, "boundaries. The processing time is only that of the injection and polymerization, which scale only weakly ")
  #line-at(54pt, 510.96pt, "with volume. Both full and hollow structures, including ones with multiple partitions, can be formed, and ")
  #line-at(54pt, 524.76pt, "in all cases the resulting surfaces exhibit sub-nanometric surface roughness without the need for post-")
  #line-at(54pt, 538.56pt, "processing. Importantly, in contrast to historical form-finding methods that were used exclusively to ")
  #line-at(54pt, 552.36pt, "design complex morphologies that were then implemented using existing tools and materials, LiquiFab ")
  #line-at(54pt, 566.16pt, "utilizes form-finding principles for the construction process itself.  ")
  #line-at(54pt, 593.76pt, "More complex structures can be formed by sequentially applying LiquiFab; once an object is created, its ")
  #line-at(54pt, 607.56pt, "edges serve as one or more boundary conditions for the next injection. Each element can be considered a ")
  #line-at(54pt, 621.36pt, "‘brick’ in the larger construct, with the assembly occurring naturally as part of the process. Arbitrary 3D ")
  #line-at(54pt, 635.16pt, "structures can be realized with this approach; we demonstrate here the ability to automate the process ")
  #line-at(54pt, 648.96pt, "using a robotic arm and create a geodesic structure.  ")
  #line-at(54pt, 676.56pt, "LiquiFab structures can be explored both physically and digitally. We provide an open source ")
  #line-at(54pt, 690.36pt, "computational framework, built on Surface Evolver")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(312.12pt, 685.8pt, "11")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(320.28pt, 690.36pt, " for predicting liquid interfaces in contact with ")
  #line-at(54pt, 704.16pt, "boundary elements and validate it experimentally using 3D scans of produced objects. The software ")
]

