#original-page(612pt, 792pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(275.76pt, 753.36pt, "Page ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(302.04pt, 753.36pt, "3")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(308.04pt, 753.36pt, " of ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(324pt, 753.36pt, "16")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(54pt, 83.16pt, "envelope runs Surface Evolver via the Rhino/Grasshopper graphical user interface, without requiring new ")
  #line-at(54pt, 96.96pt, "programming skills.  ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(54pt, 124.56pt, "Principle of the method  ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(54pt, 152.16pt, "Consider a volume of liquid polymer injected into an immiscible immersion liquid of equal density.  Under ")
  #line-at(54pt, 165.96pt, "such neutral buoyancy conditions, gravity forces are precisely countered by buoyancy such that the only ")
  #line-at(54pt, 179.76pt, "force acting on the liquid is its own surface tension. In the absence of any additional constraints, the liquid ")
  #line-at(54pt, 193.56pt, "would reach a minimum-energy state corresponding to a perfect sphere. This volume can now be ")
  #line-at(54pt, 207.36pt, "transformed into other shapes by bringing it in contact with one or more surfaces that serve as physical ")
  #line-at(54pt, 221.16pt, "constraints or mathematical boundary conditions. The liquid would then take a new shape, corresponding ")
  #line-at(54pt, 234.96pt, "to its new minimum-energy configuration that respects the imposed constraints and its total volume.  ")
  #line-at(54pt, 262.56pt, "Fig. 1a-i provides a schematic illustration of such a transformation.  A circular nozzle is used to inject a ")
  #line-at(54pt, 276.36pt, "volume of polymer into a container filled with a mixture of water and glycerol, having a density equal to ")
  #line-at(54pt, 290.16pt, "that of the polymer.  The liquid polymer is initially nearly spherical, except for its contact with the injector ")
  #line-at(54pt, 303.96pt, "surface. As the polymer comes into contact with and wets the additional surfaces, it quickly redistributes ")
  #line-at(54pt, 317.76pt, "in space and assumes a new minimum-energy state corresponding to its new geometrical constraints. ")
  #line-at(54pt, 331.56pt, "Addition or aspiration of liquid polymer modifies this minimum-energy state and thus the shape of the ")
  #line-at(54pt, 345.36pt, "surface. The polymer is then cured (either thermally or, for photopolymers, by exposure to UV light), ")
  #line-at(54pt, 359.16pt, "resulting in a solid 3D physical object. In the example here, three circular boundary conditions are used, ")
  #line-at(54pt, 372.96pt, "yet in principle, any number of boundary conditions of any shape can be employed (Supplementary Fig. ")
  #line-at(54pt, 386.76pt, "5). Importantly, the method is scale-invariant. i.e., once gravity is eliminated, the capillary length becomes ")
  #line-at(54pt, 400.56pt, "infinite, and objects of any size can be created in precisely the same way, as illustrated by the 22 cm tall ")
  #line-at(54pt, 414.36pt, "object in Fig. 1j. The process can be repeated sequentially, with the edges of one object serving as the ")
  #line-at(54pt, 428.16pt, "boundary conditions for the next one, as illustrated in Fig. 1g-i. By automating the process and repeating ")
  #line-at(54pt, 441.96pt, "it, large constructs can be rapidly fabricated. Fig. 1k presents a geodesic dome fabricated using this ")
  #line-at(54pt, 455.76pt, "approach.   ")
]

