#original-page(612pt, 792pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(275.76pt, 753.36pt, "Page ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(302.04pt, 753.36pt, "5")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(308.04pt, 753.36pt, " of ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(324pt, 753.36pt, "16")
  #line-at(54pt, 83.16pt, "Experimental  ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(54pt, 110.76pt, "Fig. 2a-d presents our fundamental mode of fabrication (Supplementary video 1), using three equally ")
  #line-at(54pt, 124.56pt, "spaced annular boundary surfaces under a neutral buoyancy environment. Details of the materials and the ")
  #line-at(54pt, 138.36pt, "experimental setup are provided in the methods section. We inject the liquid simultaneously through all ")
  #line-at(54pt, 152.16pt, "three boundaries, forming a nearly spherical ‘blob’ on each. As more liquid is injected, the blobs grow ")
  #line-at(54pt, 165.96pt, "until they come in contact. Either spontaneously or with slight agitation, the blobs coalesce into a ")
  #line-at(54pt, 179.76pt, "continuous shape corresponding to the minimum-energy state under the prescribed boundary conditions. ")
  #line-at(54pt, 193.56pt, "Importantly, any non-uniformity in the ‘blobs’ size before coalescing does not affect the final shape, which ")
  #line-at(54pt, 207.36pt, "is determined solely by boundary conditions and the total injected volume. Once the shape settles, we ")
  #line-at(54pt, 221.16pt, "initiate solidification via UV exposure for approximately 10 min. The resulting solid object can then be ")
  #line-at(54pt, 234.96pt, "easily detached from the contact surfaces, which are made of molded silicone, and removed from the ")
  #line-at(54pt, 248.76pt, "immersion liquid. The retrieved object is washed under a water stream to remove any immersion liquid ")
  #line-at(54pt, 262.56pt, "residuals and is then placed to dry in normal room conditions. This example makes use of three boundary ")
  #line-at(54pt, 276.36pt, "conditions with injection through all boundaries. Nevertheless, this approach can readily be implemented ")
  #line-at(54pt, 290.16pt, "using other injection modalities, as shown in Supplementary Fig. 4. It can be extended to a larger number ")
  #line-at(54pt, 303.96pt, "of boundaries, as shown in Supplementary Fig. 5. The method is not limited to UV curable materials and ")
  #line-at(54pt, 317.76pt, "can also be implemented with two-component polymers, as we demonstrate in Supplementary Fig. 6 using ")
  #line-at(54pt, 331.56pt, "polydimethylsiloxane (PDMS).  ")
  #line-at(54pt, 359.16pt, "Fig. 2e-h shows how the method can be adapted for the fabrication of thin-walled structures with internal ")
  #line-at(54pt, 372.96pt, "partitions (Supplementary video 2). As before, we start the process by forming three small ‘blobs’ (approx. ")
  #line-at(54pt, 386.76pt, "2 ml in volume each) on the boundaries, which we then ‘inflate’ by introducing immersion liquid through ")
  #line-at(54pt, 400.56pt, "the boundary ports. As more immersion liquid is injected, the blobs expand until they come in contact and ")
  #line-at(54pt, 414.36pt, "coalesce, forming a hollow structure with three subdivisions. The equal densities of all three liquids ")
  #line-at(54pt, 428.16pt, "(polymer, external immersion liquid, and internal immersion liquid) ensure neutral buoyancy throughout ")
  #line-at(54pt, 441.96pt, "the system, leaving surface tension as the only force governing the dynamics of the liquid interfaces. One ")
  #line-at(54pt, 455.76pt, "should, however, note that the outer envelope of the structure differs from that of the full polymer depicted ")
  #line-at(54pt, 469.56pt, "in Fig. 2a-d. This is because the thin partitions undergo an extremely slow draining process, and the ")
  #line-at(54pt, 483.36pt, "resulting solidified object therefore captures a time point in a dynamic process rather than in equilibrium. ")
  #line-at(54pt, 510.96pt, "Fig. 2i-l depicts a sequence of liquid polymer transformations due to the linear movement of a single ")
  #line-at(54pt, 524.76pt, "boundary (Supplementary video 3). As the boundary moves toward the center of the form, the liquid ")
  #line-at(54pt, 538.56pt, "adapts and settles into a new minimum-energy state.  Surprisingly, a stable shape is maintained even when ")
  #line-at(54pt, 552.36pt, "the angle between the liquid and the surface exceeds 180 degrees (Fig. 2k), resulting in a Napoleonic ")
  #line-at(54pt, 566.16pt, "Bicorn shape (Fig. 2l).  ")
]

