  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(324pt, 753.36pt, "16")
  #line-at(54pt, 471.96pt, "Fig 2:  Modes of fabrication.")
  #line-at(209.4pt, 471.96pt, "a-d, Fabrication of a homogenous object")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(421.2pt, 471.96pt, ". Polymer is simultaneously ")
  #line-at(54pt, 485.76pt, "injected into the immersion liquid from three boundaries, initially forming standalone blobs. Upon further ")
  #line-at(54pt, 499.56pt, "polymer injection, the blobs come into contact and coalesce into a single continuous body. The amount of ")
  #line-at(54pt, 513.36pt, "total polymer inflow controls the final shape of the body. Following a ten-minute cure under UV, the solid ")
  #line-at(54pt, 527.16pt, "object is complete. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(148.32pt, 527.16pt, "e-h, Fabrication of a hollow multi-chamber shape.")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(406.2pt, 527.16pt, " A small liquid polymer blob is ")
  #line-at(54pt, 540.96pt, "initially placed on each boundary. Immersion liquid is injected into the polymer blobs, forming thin-")
  #line-at(54pt, 554.76pt, "walled spheres. Upon contact, the spheres coalesce and transform into a unified body with internal ")
  #line-at(54pt, 568.56pt, "partitions. After polymerization, the inflation liquid is drained, resulting in an object with internal cavities ")
  #line-at(54pt, 582.36pt, "divided by internal walls. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(178.44pt, 582.36pt, "i-l,")
  #line-at(195pt, 582.36pt, "Fabrication by moving boundaries. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(377.52pt, 582.36pt, "For a polymer volume in contact with ")
  #line-at(54pt, 596.16pt, "all its boundaries, the displacement of boundary elements forces it to adjust to a new minimum-energy ")
  #line-at(54pt, 609.96pt, "state, while the total polymer volume in the system remains constant throughout the process. This allows ")
  #line-at(54pt, 623.76pt, "the fabrication of shapes that are not achievable with static boundaries.  ")
]

// arXiv source page 7
#original-page(612pt, 792pt, (image("graphics/page-07.png", width: 2pt), 59pt, 616.5pt))[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(275.76pt, 753.36pt, "Page ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(302.04pt, 753.36pt, "7")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(308.04pt, 753.36pt, " of ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(324pt, 753.36pt, "16")
  #line-at(54pt, 83.16pt, "Computational framework ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(54pt, 110.76pt, "Surface Evolver is a powerful computational tool developed by Ken Brakke")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(440.04pt, 106.2pt, "11")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(448.2pt, 110.76pt, " for simulation of the ")
  #line-at(54pt, 124.56pt, "minimum-energy state of liquid volumes and surfaces. Surface Evolver's robust mathematical framework ")
  #line-at(54pt, 138.36pt, "enables the incorporation of a variety of forces and boundary conditions. It has been used in a wide range ")
  #line-at(54pt, 152.16pt, "of applications, such as the behavior of liquid rocket fuel in low gravity, the shape of molten solder in ")
  #line-at(54pt, 165.96pt, "microcircuits, and the formation of triply periodic minimal surfaces. It is thus a natural choice for ")
  #line-at(54pt, 179.76pt, "simulating LiquiFab structures.  ")
  #line-at(54pt, 207.36pt, "To enable computation of complex structures and rapid iterations on design ideas, as well as to make the ")
  #line-at(54pt, 221.16pt, "tool accessible to the design community, we developed a computational envelope in Grasshopper/Rhino. ")
  #line-at(54pt, 234.96pt, "This Grasshopper-Surface Evolver (GSE) environment facilitates simple definition of boundary ")
  #line-at(54pt, 248.76pt, "conditions and liquid volumes, and allows to run the Surface Evolver computational core through a ")
  #line-at(54pt, 262.56pt, "graphical user-friendly environment (see Methods section).    ")
  #line-at(54pt, 290.16pt, "As shown in Fig. 3a, the user defines the boundary conditions by creating an arbitrary closed surface that ")
  #line-at(54pt, 303.96pt, "represents the boundaries of the injected liquid. We designed GSE to interpret any holes in this envelope ")
  #line-at(54pt, 317.76pt, "as fixed boundary conditions to which the liquid will be pinned. The precise shape of this surface is of no ")
  #line-at(54pt, 331.56pt, "consequence and can be defined as a simplistic block. The GSE then creates a Surface Evolver command ")
  #line-at(54pt, 345.36pt, "language script that includes the mesh itself, a convergence algorithm to settle the fluid into its final form,  ")
  #line-at(54pt, 359.16pt, "and a remeshing algorithm based on Bostch et al.")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(304.2pt, 354.6pt, "12")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(312.24pt, 359.16pt, " applied during the convergence process. Surface ")
  #line-at(54pt, 372.96pt, "Evolver is executed as a subprocess of the Grasshopper script. It outputs its converged result to a file, ")
  #line-at(54pt, 386.76pt, "which is then automatically reloaded into Grasshopper, enabling further handling in the ")
  #line-at(54pt, 400.56pt, "Grasshopper/Rhino environment. Fig. 3b-d show examples of such solutions obtained using the boundary ")
  #line-at(54pt, 414.36pt, "conditions depicted in Fig. 3a. In perfect neutral buoyancy, an increase in the liquid volume results in the ")
  #line-at(54pt, 428.16pt, "inflation of the original shape (Fig. 3b-c), while a slight deviation from neutral buoyancy yields out-of-")
  #line-at(54pt, 441.96pt, "plane deformations of the original shape (Fig. 3d). The run time of the simulation depends on the geometry ")
  #line-at(54pt, 455.76pt, "and the desired surface resolution but is on the order of few seconds for the examples we show here, ")
  #line-at(54pt, 469.56pt, "making it a convenient tool for iterative design.   ")
  #line-at(54pt, 497.16pt, "To validate the numerical code, we compare physical and computational objects produced under the same ")
  #line-at(54pt, 510.96pt, "conditions. Fig. 3e presents an image of a physical object we produced using the boundary conditions and ")
  #line-at(54pt, 524.76pt, "volume of the computed object in Fig. 3c. By scanning the object with a 3D scanner, we obtain its surface ")
  #line-at(54pt, 538.56pt, "topography in digital form, which we can then compare to the computed topography. Fig. 3f presents such ")
  #line-at(54pt, 552.36pt, "a comparison as a 3D colormap of the surface difference between the two (Methods section). The ")
  #line-at(54pt, 566.16pt, "differences are sub-millimetric across the entire object, with a maximum of 0.5 mm and an RMS of ")
  #line-at(54pt, 579.96pt, "0.2 mm, representing deviations of 1.2% and 0.5% of the characteristic body length, respectively. These ")
  #line-at(54pt, 593.76pt, "deviations are very reasonable and even expected, given polymer shrinkage, immersion liquid density ")
  #line-at(54pt, 607.56pt, "errors, and scanning accuracy, and thus provide confidence in the validity of the computational tool. ")
]

