// arXiv source page 9
#original-page(612pt, 792pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(275.76pt, 753.36pt, "Page ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(302.04pt, 753.36pt, "9")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(308.04pt, 753.36pt, " of ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(324pt, 753.36pt, "16")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 13.56pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(54pt, 84.6pt, "Sequential Construction from LiquiBricks ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(54pt, 112.68pt, "To fabricate more complex structures and assemblies, we start by segmenting a desired surface into planar ")
  #line-at(54pt, 126.48pt, "facets - a standard technique in computational geometry and graphics (e.g., via variational shape ")
  #line-at(54pt, 140.28pt, "approximation")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(123.96pt, 135.72pt, "13")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(132.12pt, 140.28pt, "). Each facet is then replaced by a set of virtual boundary surfaces whose geometrical ")
  #line-at(54pt, 154.08pt, "centers coincide with the centers or the edges, and whose normal is perpendicular both to the edge and to ")
  #line-at(54pt, 167.88pt, "the average normal of the facets connected by the edge. For simplicity, we use disk-shaped boundaries ")
  #line-at(54pt, 181.68pt, "with a fixed diameter, though in principle both the shape and size of the boundaries can vary within an ")
  #line-at(54pt, 195.48pt, "object. Each set of bounding surfaces defines a LiquiBrick – an object that would be formed upon injection ")
  #line-at(54pt, 209.28pt, "of a liquid volume connecting those boundaries.  Using our GSE code, we create a digital prediction for ")
  #line-at(54pt, 223.08pt, "the entire network and use it to determine the volume assigned to the LiquiBrick (which may be uniform ")
  #line-at(54pt, 236.88pt, "or vary across the geometry).  As an illustrative example, in Fig. 4a-c, we demonstrate this concept using ")
  #line-at(54pt, 250.68pt, "a geodesic dome (an icosahedral sphere with a frequency of 2), originally popularized by ")
  #line-at(54pt, 264.48pt, "architect Buckminster Fuller.")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(193.68pt, 259.92pt, "14")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(201.72pt, 264.48pt, " (Supplementary video 4). ")
  #line-at(54pt, 292.32pt, "Fig. 4d presents the system we developed to implement such structures. The system is built around a six-")
  #line-at(54pt, 306.12pt, "degree-of-freedom robotic arm, computer-controlled for precise positioning. As its end effector, the arm ")
  #line-at(54pt, 319.92pt, "holds a metal pipe fitted with a silicone nozzle. Liquid polymer is delivered to the nozzle by a computer-")
  #line-at(54pt, 333.72pt, "controlled syringe pump through a silicone line. The nozzle is submerged within an immersion liquid ")
  #line-at(54pt, 347.52pt, "contained in a glass vessel placed on a transparent plate. The plate transfers light produced by UV LEDs ")
  #line-at(54pt, 361.32pt, "from below, enabling in situ polymerization of the injected volumes. For uniform curing of completed ")
  #line-at(54pt, 375.12pt, "objects, the container is enclosed within a polymerization sphere consisting of a base and a detachable lid. ")
  #line-at(54pt, 388.92pt, "When closed, this chamber provides isotropic UV illumination over the entire construct.  ")
  #line-at(54pt, 416.64pt, "The fabrication process is outlined in Fig. 4e-g. Given two existing boundary surfaces, the robot positions ")
  #line-at(54pt, 430.44pt, "the nozzle near one of the boundaries and injects a small initial volume of polymer that wets that boundary ")
  #line-at(54pt, 444.24pt, "(Fig. 4e), and then retracts from it.  This is then repeated for the second boundary.  Next, the robot positions ")
  #line-at(54pt, 458.04pt, "itself so that the nozzle edge lies between the two boundaries and injects a prescribed volume of polymer, ")
  #line-at(54pt, 471.84pt, "which coalesces with the polymer deposited on the two wetted boundaries, forming a continuous volume ")
  #line-at(54pt, 485.64pt, "of liquid connecting the two surfaces and the nozzle (Fig. 4f). Finally, the nozzle moves to its last position ")
  #line-at(54pt, 499.44pt, "thereby shaping the polymer into the desired form (Fig 4g). The UV light is turned on for a short time (24 ")
  #line-at(54pt, 513.24pt, "s) to partially polymerize the LiquiBrick. Illuminating only for a short time ensures that only the outer ")
  #line-at(54pt, 527.04pt, "shell of the object is solidified, while its inner volume remains liquid. The nozzle then retracts, forming a ")
  #line-at(54pt, 540.84pt, "new stand-alone object. The process then repeats with the next LiquiBrick. Keeping the core of the object ")
  #line-at(54pt, 554.64pt, "in liquid form ensures facile coalescence with the liquid injection of the next object. Once the entire ")
  #line-at(54pt, 568.44pt, "structure is formed, it is exposed to UV for a longer duration until it is fully cured (Fig. 4h). ")
  #line-at(54pt, 582.24pt, "(Supplementary video 4). ")
]

